WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:10.560
On April 19th 1995 McArthur Wheeler robbed a&nbsp;
bank with his face glazed with lemon juice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:14.800
believing the juice would make his face&nbsp;
invisible to the surveillance cameras.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:18.960
He thought so because lemon juice works&nbsp;
as invisible ink on a piece of paper.

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:26.000
Police broadcasted the security camera&nbsp;
footage on the local 11 o'clock news,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:32.640
and just after midnight Arthur was arrested.&nbsp;
Incredulously he said "But I wore the juice!"

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:38.880
Baffled by this reasoning,&nbsp;
David Dunning and Justin Kruger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:44.080
two Psychologists, studied Mr Wheeler and&nbsp;
others like him . They came to the conclusion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:50.000
that people with low ability at a task tend&nbsp;
to, paradoxically, overestimate themselves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:54.080
This cognitive bias is known&nbsp;
as The Dunning–Kruger effect.

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:01.360
Let’s look at this graphically plotting one’s&nbsp;
confidence in your ability against one’s actual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:07.120
knowledge in a field. As we learn something new,&nbsp;
we are often highly confident because we know so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:13.680
little that as soon as we do know a tiny bit, we&nbsp;
think we know it all. Those who stop learning here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:18.560
maintain a false sense of mastery. Those&nbsp;
who continue learning, realize things are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:23.040
more complex and often lose motivation. And&nbsp;
the more they increase their knowledge the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:28.320
lower their confidence becomes. Many stop at&nbsp;
this stage, thinking they’ve learned nothing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:34.560
Only if we keep going can we regain confidence&nbsp;
while getting better. And at the end, we will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:39.840
full of knowledge and almost as confident&nbsp;
in our ability as right after we started.

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:47.600
In other words, if a simpleton , a good student&nbsp;
and a wise teacher were to have a public debate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:53.440
this is how things could go down. The simpleton&nbsp;
knows just a little bit, but is very confident and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:59.280
voices his opinions loud and without hesitations.&nbsp;
The student knows more, but doesn’t realize it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:05.920
because she lacks confidence. She keeps quiet. The&nbsp;
teacher is confident, but understands how complex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:12.560
things really are, hence voices his opinions with&nbsp;
reservations. In the end, the simpleton wins the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:18.000
popular vote, because he is so confident about&nbsp;
being right and people tend to trust certainty.

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:26.080
Research from North America, Europe and&nbsp;
Japan suggests that culture plays a big role.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:30.480
From assessments of one's own&nbsp;
ability to drive we know that 93%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:34.720
of Americans think they are better&nbsp;
drivers than average, while “only '' 69%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:40.720
of Swedish think so. In Japan on the other&nbsp;
hand, people, in general, tend to underestimate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:45.840
their abilities as a strategy to see their&nbsp;
underachievement as an opportunity to improve.

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:52.320
Setting out on a journey of learning&nbsp;
can be a daunting experience.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:57.360
What starts off as a leisurely stroll&nbsp;
soon changes to an intense battle of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:02.640
willpower between you and an intimidating&nbsp;
amount of knowledge. Do not give up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:07.760
The longer you fight, the more power&nbsp;
you gain, up until the point you win.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:14.080
And in the end, if you persevere, you&nbsp;
may be elevated to the ranks of Socrates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:18.240
who, over 2000 years ago,&nbsp;
left us with a quote of wisdom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:31.840
“I know that I am intelligent,&nbsp;
because I know that I know nothing.”

